- [ ] **Step 1: Predict on mount, seed the occasion — and gate generate until it resolves**

The occasion currently seeds to `"everyday"`. Predict instead, and remember the prediction so overrides can be measured against it. **Critically, the generate effect must not fire until the prediction has seeded the occasion** — otherwise on first load it generates `everyday` first, then the predicted occasion, spending TWO AI calls on a fresh day and defeating the daily-drop cost win.

```tsx
  const [reason, setReason] = useState<string>("");
  const [seeded, setSeeded] = useState(false);
  const predictedRef = useRef<UiOccasion>("everyday");

  // The morning seed: pick the predicted occasion BEFORE the first generate, so
  // the effect reads the right stored set and runs ONCE. Cheap (no AI/weather).
  useEffect(() => {
    let cancelled = false;
    predictDefaultOccasion()
      .then(({ occasion, reason }) => {
        if (cancelled) return;
        predictedRef.current = occasion;
        setReason(reason);
        setOccasion(occasion);
      })
      .finally(() => {
        // Even if prediction fails, release the gate so looks still load
        // (occasion stays at the "everyday" fallback).
        if (!cancelled) setSeeded(true);
      });
    return () => {
      cancelled = true;
    };
  }, []);
```

Then gate the existing generate effect so it waits for the seed. As its **first line**:

```tsx
    if (!seeded) return;
```

and add `seeded` to that effect's dependency array. This guarantees exactly one generate on mount, for the predicted occasion.

